{
  "rules": [
    "Regime-shift/turning-point miss: when the last 5–10 days show a sharp selloff or break below the 20–30d mean, the model stays anchored to the prior uptrend and overpredicts for ~2–3 weeks. Apply a downward level shift early in the horizon.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close in days 1–5 but become increasingly too high from ~day 7 onward (largest errors days 10–25). Use an error ramp (increasing negative correction with horizon).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a local peak (e.g., >2–3 EUR jump in a few sessions), the model extrapolates strength; true outcomes mean-revert and often reverse. Cap upside continuation and force mean reversion toward the 20–30d average.",
    "Underestimates immediate continuation only at the very start of the reversal: in the first ~1–3 days after an upswing, the model can be slightly low, but then quickly becomes too high as the true series rolls over. Use a small short-horizon uplift only if momentum is strongly positive, then fade.",
    "Volatility/dispersion mismatch: the model path is too smooth and too high; true outcomes show larger downside tails. Add asymmetric volatility adjustment: widen bands and skew corrections negative when recent returns are negative.",
    "Mean level anchoring: when history contains a recent high plateau (70–74), the model anchors there even as the market transitions to mid-60s. Re-anchor forecasts to the most recent 5–10d mean rather than the 30d mean during drawdowns."
  ]
}
